---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/advanced/cancer-biology-and-oncology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The biology ladder already taught you the cell cycle, DNA repair and apoptosis; cancer is what
    happens when those control systems are dismantled inside a single lineage of your own cells. The
    frame that organises everything else is <strong>somatic evolution</strong>: a tumour is a
    population of cells competing under mutation, selection and drift, evolving inside a patient in
    real time. This one idea explains cancer's age distribution (evolution needs time and sequential
    mutations), its resistance to single drugs (resistant clones pre-exist and are amplified by
    treatment), and why therapy has migrated from poison-the-dividing-cell to precisely identify-
    and-engage mechanisms.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the hallmarks, the treatments matched to mechanisms, and the screening logic" widget="equationStep" config={cancerCfg} />

  <h2>The genetic engine: oncogenes and tumour suppressors</h2>
  <p>
    Cancer genes come in two antithetical classes, and the logic of each is examinable to the base
    pair. <strong>Proto-oncogenes</strong> are the accelerator: growth-factor receptors (HER2), signal
    transducers (RAS), transcription factors (MYC). Mutation makes them dangerous by
    <em>gain of function</em> — a point mutation can convert RAS into a GTPase that never hydrolyses
    GTP, shouting "divide" permanently. Because one hyperactive copy suffices, oncogenic mutations are
    <em>dominant</em> at the cellular level. <strong>Tumour suppressors</strong> are the brakes — p53
    (guardian of the genome: halts the cycle, forces repair or apoptosis after damage), RB (the G1
    checkpoint gatekeeper), BRCA1/2 (homologous recombination repair). They fail by <em>loss of
    function</em>, and because one working copy still brakes, both must go: Knudson's
    <strong>two-hit hypothesis</strong>. Knudson's original evidence is a beautiful piece of
    quantitative reasoning: children with the inherited form of retinoblastoma present earlier, with
    bilateral, multifocal tumours (first hit inherited, one somatic hit remains), while sporadic cases
    are later, unilateral, single (both hits must occur by chance in one cell). The incidence curves
    fit the two-hit maths almost exactly — epidemiology discovering a molecular rule decades before
    the gene was cloned.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Worked example on risk.</strong> BRCA1 carriers face lifetime breast-cancer risks around
    55–72% (population baseline ~12%) and ovarian risks of ~39–44% — which is why testing drives
    surveillance and preventive-surgery decisions. Yet hereditary cases are a minority: roughly 5–10%
    of cancers run in strong single-gene families. The rest is somatic: 40-plus years of division,
    with each cell division carrying a small mutation rate, plus exogenous accelerants (tobacco's
    benzo[a]pyrene adducts, UV thymine dimers, HPV's E6/E7 proteins that sabotage p53 and RB). The
    correlation between lifetime normal stem-cell divisions and cancer incidence across tissues
    (Tomasetti and Vogelstein's work) quantifies how much risk is bad luck in replication — while the
    large residual differences between populations quantify how much remains preventable. Both are true.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "cancer is one disease with one cure waiting to be found." There
    are hundreds of diseases with distinct driver combinations; lung cancer with an EGFR mutation and
    lung cancer without it are therapeutically different planets. The unifying abstraction is the
    evolutionary process, not the pathology — which is exactly why "the cure for cancer" is a category
    error and why progress arrives cancer-by-cancer, target-by-target.
  </p>

  <h2>Metastasis: the cascade that kills</h2>
  <p>
    Over 90% of cancer deaths are metastasis-driven, not primary-tumour-driven. The cascade has
    defined, lossy steps: local <strong>invasion</strong> (cells degrade basement membrane with
    matrix metalloproteinases and adopt motile programs), <strong>intravasation</strong> into blood
    or lymphatics, <strong>survival in circulation</strong> (shear stress, immune patrol — most cells
    die within hours), <strong>extravasation</strong> through distant endothelium, and
    <strong>colonisation</strong> — founding a population in alien tissue, the hardest step and the
    rate limiter. Colonisation explains organ tropism (the seed-and-soil hypothesis: breast cancer
    seeds bone, liver, lung and brain because those soils provide the survival factors particular
    clones require). Quantitatively, the cascade is brutal: a primary tumour may shed millions of
    cells per gram per day, yet clinical metastases arise from a vanishing fraction — which is also
    why <strong>stage</strong> (T: tumour size/invasion, N: nodes involved, M: metastasis) is the
    strongest single prognostic variable in oncology. Five-year survival for many cancers runs from
    the 90s at stage I to single digits at stage IV; catching disease pre-metastatic is the biggest
    lever medicine holds.
  </p>

  <h2>Angiogenesis and the hallmarks, connected</h2>
  <p>
    A tumour beyond about a millimetre hits the diffusion limit for oxygen and nutrients — the same
    physics that limits anything living without plumbing. Cancers escape by inducing angiogenesis,
    tipping the balance of pro- and anti-angiogenic signals (VEGF up, thrombospondin down). Judah
    Folkman's hypothesis became a drug class: bevacizumab (anti-VEGF antibody) and small-molecule
    VEGF-receptor inhibitors are now standard components of several regimens. The clinical lesson is
    more nuanced than the theory: shutting angiogenesis rarely eradicates disease, but it can normalise
    and prune the chaotic vasculature, improving delivery of chemotherapy and slowing growth — a
    hallmark exploited as a component, not a cure. Walk the full hallmark list with this lens:
    each capability (evading apoptosis, replicative immortality via telomerase, metabolic reprogramming
    to aerobic glycolysis — the Warburg effect — immune evasion) is a potential point of pharmacological
    attack, and the 2022 update added phenotypic plasticity and polymorphic microbial communities to
    the list.
  </p>

  <h2>Targeted therapy: mechanism matched to mutation</h2>
  <p>
    The paradigm case is chronic myeloid leukaemia. CML cells carry the Philadelphia chromosome — a
    reciprocal 9;22 translocation fusing BCR to ABL, producing a constitutively active tyrosine
    kinase. Imatinib was designed into that kinase's ATP-binding pocket, inhibiting the fusion protein
    and essentially nothing else. The results reset the field: chronic-phase patients went from
    median survival of a few years (interferon-era) to five-year survival around 90% on a daily pill —
    disease controlled, not eradicated, because leukaemic stem cells persist below detection.
    Genotype-before-treatment is now standard across oncology: EGFR mutations and ALK fusions dictate
    kinase-inhibitor choice in lung cancer; HER2 amplification dictates trastuzumab in breast cancer;
    BRCA-mutated tumours' repair defect is exploited by PARP inhibitors (synthetic lethality — a cell
    with one broken repair pathway tolerates one drug, but a cell with two cannot survive). Resistance,
    predictably, evolves: ABL's "gatekeeper" T315I mutation blocks imatinib binding, answered by
    second-generation inhibitors — the drug-resistance arms race in its cleanest form.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Immunotherapy</strong> is the second modern pillar. T cells carry checkpoints (PD-1) that
    tumours hijack via ligands (PD-L1) to suppress killing; blocking antibodies (pembrolizumab,
    nivolumab) release the brake, producing durable — sometimes decade-long — remissions in melanoma
    and lung and kidney cancer (Nobel Prize 2018, Allison and Honjo). CAR-T cell therapy goes further,
    reprogramming a patient's T cells with a synthetic receptor against CD19; approved since 2017 for
    refractory leukaemias and lymphomas, with real complete remissions in end-stage disease and real
    toxicities (cytokine-release syndrome) to manage. Caveats to hold alongside the enthusiasm:
    checkpoint inhibitors help a minority of unselected patients (biomarkers like PD-L1 and
    tumour-mutational burden predict who), and autoimmunity is the physiological price of releasing
    the brakes.
  </p>

  <h2>Screening: the epidemiological trap</h2>
  <p>
    Early detection looks self-evidently good, and sometimes is. But the numbers demand care. Two
    biases inflate apparent benefit. <strong>Lead-time bias</strong>: screening advances the date of
    diagnosis without changing the date of death. A cancer found at 60 with death at 70 shows 5-year
    survival; the same cancer screen-detected at 55 shows 15-year survival — with nothing gained.
    <strong>Overdiagnosis</strong>: screening preferentially finds slow, indolent tumours (fast ones
    announce themselves between screens), counting as "cured" cancers that would never have harmed.
    Prostate-specific antigen screening exemplifies both: the ERSPC trial showed a modest
    prostate-cancer mortality reduction bought with substantial overdiagnosis, while US incidence
    statistics show grade-shift without proportional mortality movement. This is why honest screening
    claims are made in <em>mortality</em> (deaths prevented per 1,000 screened over 10 years), not
    survival-from-diagnosis, and why guidelines are risk- and age-stratified. Mammography, HPV testing
    and faecal-immunochemical colonoscopy triage sit on that spectrum with very different
    benefit-harm ratios.
  </p>

  <h2>A tumour's timeline: the worked natural history</h2>
  <p>
    Assemble the pieces into a plausible sequence. A bronchial epithelial cell in a smoker's
    lung acquires an oncogenic KRAS mutation; years later a TP53 hit removes the apoptosis
    brake; the clone proliferates, and mutation bursts (chromosomal shattering, APOBEC
    activity) diversify it into subclones. By the time a 1 cm lesion exists — around
    10<sup>9</sup> cells — the tumour has been evolving for a decade or more, angiogenesis has
    been recruited, and metastatic competence may already exist in a minority clone. Symptoms
    and diagnosis typically arrive at 10<sup>9</sup>–10<sup>11</sup> cells; by then "early
    detection" is late in evolutionary terms, which is why screening targets the interval and
    why liquid biopsies (catching circulating tumour DNA shed at 10<sup>7</sup>–10<sup>8</sup>
    cell equivalents) are the detection frontier. The timeline also explains therapy's
    evolutionary pressure: cytotoxic chemotherapy prunes sensitive subclones and selects
    resistant ones, so adjuvant therapy targets micrometastases while the tumour is still a
    small, relatively homogeneous population — the logic of treating hardest when diversity is
    lowest.
  </p>

  <h2>Surgery, radiation, and the local-versus-systemic divide</h2>
  <p>
    The treatment triad deserves its mechanisms stated. <strong>Surgery</strong> remains the
    single most curative modality — complete resection with clear margins is the only therapy
    that eradicates a tumour's full heterogeneity at once — but works only where disease is
    localised, which is why staging (imaging, biopsy, sometimes lymph-node dissection)
    precedes it. <strong>Radiotherapy</strong> kills by ionising DNA: fractionated doses
    exploit normal tissue's repair advantage over tumour cells (sublethal-damage repair
    between fractions), with intensity-modulated delivery sculpting dose to millimetres;
    combined with surgery it cleans margins and nodal basins. <strong>Systemic therapy</strong>
    — the chemotherapy, targeted and immune modalities above — is the only arm that reaches
    disease beyond the surgeon's reach, and its curative uses (testicular cancer, many
    childhood leukaemias, Hodgkin lymphoma) are among oncology's proudest results. The modern
    specialty is the choreography: which sequence of local and systemic arms, for which stage
    and molecular profile — a decision architecture the multidisciplinary tumour board
    institutionalises.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Intratumour heterogeneity and clonal dynamics.</strong> Sequencing shows a tumour is a
    branching tree of subclones; biopsies sample one twig. Spatial and temporal heterogeneity explain
    treatment failure better than any single-gene model, and evolutionary therapy — scheduling drugs
    to <em>manage</em> rather than maximally shrink tumour burden, keeping sensitive competitors alive
    to suppress resistant clones — is the serious research programme (promising trials in prostate
    cancer). <strong>Liquid biopsy</strong>: circulating tumour DNA offers real-time, non-invasive
    monitoring of the clone tree — enabling minimal-residual-disease detection months before
    radiographic relapse. <strong>Mutational signatures</strong>: each carcinogen and repair defect
    leaves a characteristic base-substitution fingerprint in the genome (UV's C-to-T signature,
    APOBEC bursts, HR-deficiency scars), turning every tumour into a retrospective exposure history.
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Why do some cancers metastasise deterministically to specific
    organs while others never do? Can dormancy — metastatic cells sitting quiescent for decades — be
    pharmacologically maintained rather than killed? Does the microbiome shape immunotherapy response,
    and how? And the inequality problem: five-year survival in high-income countries runs about twice
    that in low-income settings for the same diseases, a gap made of screening, pathology and drug
    access rather than biology — oncology's frontier is as much delivery as discovery.
  </p>
  <p>
    A final calibration for the informed reader: survival statistics improve for two distinct
    reasons — real therapeutic progress and stage migration (earlier diagnosis moves patients
    into better-prognosis buckets without changing biology). The honest reading of five-year
    survival curves separates the two, and the genuinely hard-won progress — childhood
    leukaemia, testicular cancer, CML — sits alongside screen-detected increments that are
    partly accounting. Both count; only one is a cure rate.
  </p>

